{"sounds":{"intro":"Swahili is spelled as it sounds. The stress is always on the second-last syllable. It has five pure vowels, like Spanish. Verbs are built from small pieces: who does it, when, then the verb stem. \"Ninakula\" is ni (I) + na (now) + kula (eat) = I am eating. Nouns are grouped into classes, and the class changes the words that agree with them.","rows":[["a","like \"a\" in \"father\"","baba"],["e","like \"e\" in \"bed\"","nenda"],["i","like \"ee\" in \"see\"","simba"],["o","like \"o\" in \"more\", but short","moja"],["u","like \"oo\" in \"moon\"","kuku"],["ch","like \"ch\" in \"church\"","chai"],["dh","like \"th\" in \"this\"","dhahabu"],["gh","a rough throat sound","ghali"],["ng'","like \"ng\" in \"singer\", with no \"g\" sound","ng'ombe"],["ny","like \"ny\" in \"canyon\"","nyama"],["mb / nd / ng","said as one sound, with a quick \"m\" or \"n\" first","mbwa"]],"notes":["Words with the m- prefix at the start, like \"mbwa\" (dog) or \"mtu\" (person), have a short \"m\" sound before the next letter. Do not add a vowel.","Swahili is spoken in Tanzania, Kenya and the region around them. The standard is Tanzanian, with \"Kiswahili\" the name of the language itself."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","sifuri","see-FOO-ree"],["one","moja","MOH-jah"],["two","mbili","MBEE-lee"],["three","tatu","TAH-too"],["four","nne","NNEH"],["five","tano","TAH-noh"],["six","sita","SEE-tah"],["seven","saba","SAH-bah"],["eight","nane","NAH-neh"],["nine","tisa","TEE-sah"],["ten","kumi","KOO-mee"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","kumi na moja","KOO-mee nah MOH-jah"],["twelve","kumi na mbili","KOO-mee nah MBEE-lee"],["thirteen","kumi na tatu","KOO-mee nah TAH-too"],["fourteen","kumi na nne","KOO-mee nah NNEH"],["fifteen","kumi na tano","KOO-mee nah TAH-noh"],["sixteen","kumi na sita","KOO-mee nah SEE-tah"],["seventeen","kumi na saba","KOO-mee nah SAH-bah"],["eighteen","kumi na nane","KOO-mee nah NAH-neh"],["nineteen","kumi 